<header class="sg-head"><p class="featured-region">Work song · 1867 · Americas</p><h1 class="sg-title">Blow the Man Down</h1><p class="sg-kid" data-say>A sailors' work song of the 1860s: one man sang a line and the crew answered as they hauled the ropes to raise the sails.</p></header>
<dl class="sg-facts"><div><dt>Words</dt><dd>traditional</dd></div><div><dt>Music</dt><dd>traditional</dd></div><div><dt>Culture</dt><dd>Sailors of British and American merchant ships</dd></div><div><dt>Language</dt><dd>English</dd></div><div><dt>Place</dt><dd>North Atlantic sailing and steam ships (New York, Liverpool and Glasgow routes)</dd></div><div><dt>Date</dt><dd>by the 1860s; quoted in print 1867</dd></div><div><dt>Type</dt><dd>Work song</dd></div><div><dt>The words</dt><dd>Public domain (traditional sea shanty; known by the 1860s)</dd></div></dl>
<div class="sg-body">
<section class="sg-sec" id="story"><h2>The story</h2><p>&quot;<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/blow-the-man-down">Blow the Man Down</a>&quot; is an English-language sea shanty, number 2624 in the Roud Folk <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/song-dynasty">Song</a> Index. Publications of the time and the memories of sailors printed later put it in use by the 1860s. In July 1867 the Syracuse Daily Courier quoted a line of it, said to be sung for hauling the halyards on a steamship bound from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-york">New York</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/glasgow">Glasgow</a>.</p><p>In 1879 George Haswell, a passenger on a steamship from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sydney-nova-scotia">Sydney</a>, wrote down some of the crew's shanties, and they were printed in the ship's own fortnightly newspaper, The Parramatta Sun, with a full set of words for &quot;<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/blow-the-man-down">Blow the Man Down</a>&quot;. Haswell's version is about a ship of the Black Ball Line. His article was little read, but it reached Laura Alexandrine Smith, whose large collection of sailors' songs, The Music of the Waters (1888), was one of the first to be widely available. Smith reprinted Haswell's words and gave a second version, presumably collected by herself, said to be sung for hoisting the topsail yards, in which a sailor comes home to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hong-kong">Hong Kong</a> and meets a girl on &quot;<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/winchester">Winchester</a> Street&quot;.</p><p>Like most shanties of its kind, it had no fixed text. The shantyman sang solo verses, some customary, some floating from one shanty to another and some made up on the spot, and the crew answered each with an unchanging two-part refrain, so the song could run as long as the work required. Felix Riesenberg, who trained and served as an officer in the Merchant Marine in the 1890s, described earlier sailors singing plainer working verses, not about the Black Ball Line, while raising the topsail of a merchant ship to sail from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-york">New York</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liverpool">Liverpool</a>, led by a sailor named Jimmie.</p><p>Many recordings of former sailors singing it were made in the first half of the 20th century. Percy Grainger recorded a man named Tom Roberts singing a version in Chelsea, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a>, in 1908, now in the British Library Sound Archive; the folklorist <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/james-madison">James Madison</a> Carpenter recorded it in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scotland">Scotland</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wales">Wales</a> in the early 1930s, recordings kept by the Vaughan Williams Memorial Library; Helen Hartness <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/flanders">Flanders</a> recorded versions by old sailors in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-england">New England</a> in the 1940s, and Helen Creighton in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nova-scotia">Nova Scotia</a> in the 1940s and 1950s.</p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="meaning"><h2>What it says</h2><p>A halyard shanty, made to time a crew's pulls on the ropes. In its best-known couplets a sailor walking down Paradise Street meets a pretty girl, who steers him to a fine ship bound for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-york">New York</a>; once at sea it proves a Black Ball packet, where the mate knocks him down with a spar, and he ends by warning his hearers never to heed what pretty girls say. Other couplets describe the landsmen who ship aboard the Black Ball and the hard orders that greet them. Every couplet is answered by the crew's refrain; the mood is rough, rhythmic and wry.</p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="words"><h2>The words</h2><div class="sg-blocks"><div class="sg-lyrics sg-cols-1"><div class="sg-colhead" aria-hidden="true"><span class="sg-o">The words</span></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>As I was a walking down Paradise Street</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>A pretty young damsel I chanced for to meet.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>She was round in the counter and bluff in the bow,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>So I took in all sail and cried, &quot;Way enough now.&quot;</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>I hailed her in English, she answered me clear,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>&quot;I'm from the Black Arrow bound to the Shakespeare.&quot;</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>So I tailed her my flipper and took her in tow</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>And yardarm to yardarm away we did go.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>But as we were going she said unto me</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>There's a spanking full-rigger just ready for sea.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>That spanking full-rigger to New York was bound;</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>She was very well manned and very well found.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>And as soon as that packet was out on the sea,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>'Twas devilish hard treatment of every degree.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>But as soon as that packet was clear of the bar</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>The mate knocked me down with the end of a spar.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>It's starboard and larboard on deck you will sprawl</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>For Kicking Jack Williams commands the Black Ball.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>So I give you fair warning before we belay,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Don't ever take heed of what pretty girls say.</span></p></div></div></div><div class="sg-credit"><p>Words: Traditional; 19th-century sailors' couplets as quoted in Wikipedia's article. Text from <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Blow_the_Man_Down" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikipedia</a>; public domain.</p></div></section>
